--------------------------------------------------------------------
CRITERION 1 — the stale-read bug is real and is fixed.  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Reverted the fix in the scratch tree with a 4-hunk mutation restoring the
pre-fix semantics exactly as described by 06176ef's own diff (git diff in the
worktree, "+1 -6"):
  - publishCache: `if s.cacheGen != gen { return }` replaced by `_ = gen`
    (pre-fix publish was an unconditional `s.cache[path] = entry`)
  - writeFileLocked: post-atomicWrite `s.invalidateCache(path)` deleted
  - CreateExclusive: post-createExclusive `s.invalidateCache(path)` deleted
  - removeLocked: post-os.Remove `s.invalidateCache(path)` deleted

PRE-FIX (scratch tree):
  $ go test -race -count=1 -run TestReadFileConcurrentWriterNeverServesStale \
      ./internal/services/storage/
  --- FAIL: TestReadFileConcurrentWriterNeverServesStale (0.00s)
      cache_test.go:92: round 2: ReadFile returned "BBBB" after
      WriteFile("AAAA") returned; the cache is serving stale data
  FAIL  budget2/internal/services/storage  0.011s   EXIT=1
  Repeated three more times: failed at round 28, round 4, round 4 — always
  the same assertion at cache_test.go:92. The failure is the exact symptom
  the commit message describes (Storage.ReadFile disagreeing with the write
  that had already returned).

POST-FIX (master @ 5eeca01):
  $ go test -race -count=3 -run 'TestReadFileConcurrentWriterNeverServesStale|
      TestPublishCache|TestRemoveOrdersCacheAgainstUnlink|TestLock' -v ./...storage/
  --- PASS: TestReadFileConcurrentWriterNeverServesStale (0.07s) x3   EXIT=0

Discrepancy recorded, not treated as failure: the commit claims "fails on the
old code at round 1"; observed first failure was at round 2, 28, 4, 4 across
four runs. The test is a race stress loop, so the round index is inherently
non-deterministic. The claim's substance — the test fails against the pre-fix
code, quickly, under -race — is demonstrated. The precise number is not.

I read the test before citing it (internal/services/storage/cache_test.go:29-107).
It does what its name says: 4 concurrent reader goroutines, a writer alternating
two EQUAL-LENGTH payloads ("AAAA"/"BBBB") with os.Chtimes pinning mtime to a
frozen value after every write, and the assertion is a serial read taken while
the writer is quiescent. The equal length + frozen mtime are what defeat the
mtime+size staleness key, which is precisely the condition the bug needs.

