CRITERION 4 — Lock bumps the generation too.  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Deleted only `s.cacheGen++` from Lock (storage.go:228); one-line diff shown by
git diff in the worktree.

  $ go test -race -count=1 -run TestLockBarsPublishFromReadStartedBeforeLock \
      -v ./internal/services/storage/
      cache_test.go:326: a read started before Lock put plaintext back into
                         the cleared cache: "AAAA"
      cache_test.go:330: ReadFile succeeded while the store was locked
  --- FAIL: TestLockBarsPublishFromReadStartedBeforeLock (7.93s)   EXIT=1

Both counts the commit names fail, in the order it names them: the entry lands
(line 326), and the follow-up ReadFile then succeeds against a locked store
(line 330), serving plaintext the lock was supposed to have removed. On master
the same test passes (see criterion 1's -count=3 run).

I also checked 79ef006's own disclaimer, since it is a claim in the commit
message: with Lock's bump removed,
  $ go test -race -count=1 -run TestLockUnderConcurrentReadsLeavesNoPlaintext -v
  --- PASS: TestLockUnderConcurrentReadsLeavesNoPlaintext (5.35s)   EXIT=0
The commit says this test "passes without the bump ... it guards the property,
it does not reproduce the gap." That is accurate — the author disclosed the
weaker test rather than passing it off as the reproduction.

